Aim
To create an AWS Lambda function that automatically triggers whenever an object is uploaded to an Amazon S3 bucket and stores the uploaded object metadata into a DynamoDB table. 

Procedure
Step 1: Login to AWS Console


Open Amazon Web Services AWS Management Console.


Login using AWS credentials. 



Step 2: Create an S3 Bucket


Go to:


Services → S3




Click Create Bucket.


Enter bucket name.


Select AWS region.


Click Create Bucket.



Step 3: Create DynamoDB Table


Go to:


Services → DynamoDB




Click Create Table.


Configure:


Table Name:
newtable2


Partition Key:
unique




Click Create Table.



Step 4: Open AWS Lambda Console


Go to:


Services → Lambda




Click Create Function. 



Step 5: Create Lambda Function


Select:


Author from scratch




Configure:


Function Name:
S3ToDynamoDB


Runtime:
Python




Under Permissions:


Create new role with basic Lambda permissions




Click Create Function. 



Step 6: Add Lambda Function Code
Replace default code with:
import boto3from uuid import uuid4def lambda_handler(event, context):    dynamodb = boto3.resource('dynamodb')    dynamoTable = dynamodb.Table('newtable2')    if 'Records' in event:        for record in event['Records']:            bucket_name = record['s3']['bucket']['name']            object_key = record['s3']['object']['key']            size = record['s3']['object'].get('size', -1)            event_name = record['eventName']            event_time = record['eventTime']            dynamoTable.put_item(                Item={                    'unique': str(uuid4()),                    'Bucket': bucket_name,                    'Object': object_key,                    'Size': size,                    'Event': event_name,                    'EventTime': event_time                }            )    else:        print("No S3 event received")    return {        "statusCode": 200,        "body": "Data stored in DynamoDB"    }
Click Deploy. 

Step 7: Configure S3 Trigger


In Lambda Function Designer section:


Click Add Trigger




Select:


S3




Configure:


Choose created S3 bucket


Event Type:
All object create events




Enable trigger.


Click Add. 



Step 8: Configure Permissions


Go to:


Configuration → Permissions




Ensure Lambda Role has:


S3 access permissions


DynamoDB write permissions





Step 9: Test the Lambda Function


Upload any file into S3 bucket.


Example:
image.png


Lambda function triggers automatically.



Step 10: Verify DynamoDB Entries


Open DynamoDB Console.


Open table:
newtable2


Click:


Explore Table Items




Verify uploaded object details appear in table. 



Step 11: Monitor Lambda Execution


Open Lambda Function.


Go to:


Monitor tab




View:


Invocation count


Logs


CloudWatch metrics




You can also open CloudWatch logs for troubleshooting. 

Observation


S3 bucket created successfully.


DynamoDB table created successfully.


Lambda function triggered automatically after object upload.


Uploaded object metadata stored in DynamoDB table.


CloudWatch logs showed successful Lambda execution. 



Result
Successfully created an AWS Lambda function that triggers automatically on S3 object uploads and stores uploaded object metadata into a DynamoDB table. 
